/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each node has a CAN controller that builds and checks frames and a transceiver that drives the twisted pair, CAN_H and CAN_L.
   - If one node drives dominant while another leaves the bus recessive, the bus is dominant.
   - No bit of the winning frame is damaged, so this is non-destructive arbitration, and a lower identifier means a higher priority: brake and engine messages get low identifiers.
   - A standard data frame has these fields, in order:
   - Bit stuffing keeps edges on the wire for synchronisation: after five equal bits in a row the transmitter adds one bit of the opposite value, and every receiver removes it.
   - Each node checks for five errors: bit (a sender reads back a different bit, outside arbitration), stuff (six equal bits), CRC, form (a fixed bit such as a delimiter is wrong) and ACK (no receiver acknowledged).
   - SP is the sample point as a fraction of one bit; a bit has N time quanta: one sync quantum, PROP for propagation delay, PHASE1 and PHASE2 either side of the sample point, N = 1 + PROP + PHASE1 + PHASE2.
   - CAN FD keeps the identifier and arbitration rules, so priority works exactly as before, and changes the control field.
   - DLC 0 to 8 means that many bytes; 9 to 15 mean 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 48 and 64 bytes.
*/
