/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 1000-byte block (a camera thumbnail, say) must cross the car. Ignoring protocol overhead, how long does it take on 19.2 kbit/s LIN, 500 kbit/s CAN, one 10 Mbit/s FlexRay channel and 100 Mbit/s Ethernet?
   1. Bits to send: 1000 × 8 = 8000 bits.
   2. LIN: 8000 ÷ 19 200 = 0.4167 s = 416.7 ms.
   3. CAN: 8000 ÷ 500 000 = 0.016 s = 16 ms.
   4. FlexRay: 8000 ÷ 10 000 000 = 0.0008 s = 0.8 ms.
   5. Ethernet: 8000 ÷ 100 000 000 = 0.00008 s = 80 µs.
   6. Ratio LIN to Ethernet: 416.7 ms ÷ 0.08 ms ≈ 5208.
   Answer: 416.7 ms on LIN, 16 ms on CAN, 0.8 ms on FlexRay and 80 µs on Ethernet; real times are longer because of headers, stuffing and gaps, but the ratio of about 5000 between LIN and Ethernet stands. */
